            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contributions to Galaxy Rotation</strong><br>\nIn the 1960s and 1970s, Vera Rubin's observations and calculations shed light on the rotation of spiral galaxies. She discovered that the stars in these galaxies rotated at similar speeds, regardless of their distance from the galactic center. This was contrary to the expected outcome that stars on the periphery would move slower than those near the center, according to the laws of physics as understood at the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Dark Matter Hypothesis</strong><br>\nThe discrepancy in galaxy rotation speeds led Rubin to consider the possibility of dark matter. Her work provided some of the most compelling evidence for the existence of this unseen matter. Dark matter is thought to make up the majority of the universe's mass and is key to understanding the gravitational forces that govern galaxy formation and stability.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Challenges in Astronomy</strong><br>\nVera Rubin's work came at a time when the scientific community was largely skeptical of the presence of dark matter. She faced resistance and doubt from some colleagues, but her persistence and thorough research provided undeniable evidence that supported her conclusions. Her findings encouraged the astronomical community to rethink traditional theories of cosmic dynamics.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
