          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Women in Science Advocacy</strong><br>\nThroughout her career, Rubin was an advocate for women in science. She was acutely aware of the gender disparities in her field and worked to support other women astronomers, often discussing her own experiences of discrimination. Her efforts contributed to increasing the representation of women in astronomical research and other scientific disciplines.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Honors and Recognition</strong><br>\nRubin's groundbreaking work on galaxy rotation and dark matter brought her many accolades. She was awarded the National Medal of Science in 1993 and was elected to the National Academy of Sciences. Her legacy is celebrated in numerous recognitions, including the naming of Rubin Observatory in her honor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nVera Rubin passed away on December 25, 2016, but her legacy endures. She transformed our understanding of the universe and inspired future generations of astronomers. Her research into the rotation of galaxies and the advocacy for women in science has had a lasting impact on both the field of astronomy and the fight for equality in the scientific community.</p>\n"
              },
